# Cursor AI Coding Rules - 90% Per-File Unit Test Coverage

- Whenever you modify, refactor, or add code to any file, ALWAYS add/update unit tests.
- Every single file must reach >= 90% unit test coverage across all 4 parameters:
  - Statements >= 90%
  - Branches >= 90%
  - Functions >= 90%
  - Lines >= 90%
- Never skip any file.
- The test configuration enforces `perFile: true` and will throw an error if coverage on any file is below 90%.
- A global timeout of 10000ms is enforced for all unit tests.
- After any code change, verify coverage by running:
  `npm run test:coverage`
- Do not stop until all tests pass and >= 90% per-file coverage is achieved.

# Centralized Structured Logging Rules
- No raw `console.log` / `console.error` calls.
- Use `logger` from `backend/src/utils/logger.ts` or `frontendLogger` from `frontend/src/utils/logger.ts`.
- Include structured context: `timestamp`, `level`, `service`, `message`, `requestId`, `context`, `durationMs`, and serialized `error` stack.
- Local persistence: All logs persist to the file system (`logs/app.log`, `logs/error.log`, `logs/app-YYYY-MM-DD.log`) when running locally.
- Compliance: Automatically purge logs older than the retention window (14 days default).
- Apply detailed logs across all controllers, services, database queries, and middleware.

# CI/CD Pipeline & Pull Request Rules
- CI/CD workflow `.github/workflows/ci.yml` runs on pull requests to validate linting, typechecking, building, and 90% per-file unit test coverage.
- All CI/CD workflows and jobs must enforce an explicit timeout (`timeout-minutes: 15`).
- The workflow must post and update an executive summary PR comment with unit test pass/fail counts, execution duration, and overall code coverage.

# User Prompt History Rules
- Maintain `prompts.md` in the workspace root for saving user-provided prompts.
- Save ONLY user-provided prompts in `prompts.md`. Do NOT save AI-generated conversations, responses, or thoughts.
- Append each user prompt in chronological sequence with clean headers.

# Internationalization (i18n) & UI_STRINGS Rules
- Never hardcode user-facing literal strings in components/JSX.
- Move all literal strings to `frontend/src/constants/uiStrings.ts` accessible through `UI_STRINGS`.
- Use template placeholders/functions for dynamic runtime substitution.
- Update tests to assert against `UI_STRINGS` constants rather than literal strings.
- Enforce >= 90% unit test coverage on all constants and helper modules.

# Separate Constants File Rules
- Keep all application constants, defaults, lookup tables, and palettes in separate files under `constants/`.
- Never inline magic numbers or configuration dictionaries directly in components, services, or controllers.
- Group constants logically by domain (`app.ts`, `currency.ts`, `pipeline.ts`, `logger.ts`) and export via `constants/index.ts`.
- Enforce >= 90% unit test coverage on all constants files.

# Separate Data Types & Interfaces Rules
- Keep all data types, interfaces, enums, and type aliases in separate files under `types/`.
- Never declare `interface` or `type` definitions directly in component (`.tsx`), controller, or service files.
- Move all component & modal prop interfaces to dedicated type files (e.g. `types/components.ts`) and import them.
- Re-export all types via `types/index.ts`.

# Input Schema Validation Rules
- Enforce strict schema validation for all user and system inputs (forms, modals, API routes, controller bodies, query parameters, headers).
- Define all validation schemas in dedicated constants modules under `constants/` (`frontend/src/constants/validation.ts` and `backend/src/constants/validation.ts`).
- Never declare validation schemas inline.
- Keep derived types in `types/validation.ts` re-exported via `types/index.ts`.
- Reject invalid inputs immediately with HTTP 400 Bad Request.
- Maintain >= 90% unit test coverage for all validation schemas and utilities.

# Comprehensive & Descriptive UI Error Messaging Rules
- Always present user-facing error messages with actionable context and specific failure details across error categories (`VALIDATION_ERROR`, `NETWORK_ERROR`, `AUTH_ERROR`, `NOT_FOUND_ERROR`, `CONFLICT_ERROR`, `SERVER_ERROR`, `RATE_LIMIT_ERROR`).
- Never use generic messages like "An error occurred".
- Move all user-facing error strings to `UI_STRINGS` (`frontend/src/constants/uiStrings.ts`).
- Keep error constants in `constants/` and error types in `types/`.
- Log all UI errors via centralized logger with context and `requestId`.
- Enforce >= 90% unit test coverage on all error utilities and components.

# Database Optimization & GraphQL Integration Rules
- Audit all database queries for duration and rows affected. Emit warning logs for queries exceeding `SLOW_QUERY_THRESHOLD_MS` (100ms).
- Implement query caching with TTL for stable reads with automatic mutation invalidation to minimize database compute hours and resource usage.
- Add database indexes in Prisma schema to eliminate full-table scans.
- Provide `/api/graphql` endpoint exposing application queries and mutations. Support composite queries (e.g. `dashboardOverview`) for single round-trip data fetching.
- Keep GraphQL SDL and query strings in `constants/graphql.ts` and `constants/db.ts`, and types in `types/graphql.ts` and `types/db.ts`.
- Enforce >= 90% unit test coverage on all database and GraphQL utilities.

## 13. AES Encryption & Data Protection Policy
- Protect all sensitive data at rest and in transit using authenticated AES-256-GCM.
- Use a cryptographically secure 12-byte initialization vector (IV) generated fresh for every operation. Nonce reuse is forbidden.
- Verify the 16-byte authentication tag upon every decryption.
- Manage 256-bit keys via secure configuration or PBKDF2/SHA-256 key derivation.
- Isolate constants in `constants/crypto.ts` and types in `types/crypto.ts`. Never log plaintext keys or secrets.
- Enforce >= 90% unit test code coverage on all cryptographic utilities.

## 14. Automated Tech Stack & Libraries Upgrade Policy
- Automatically identify, audit, upgrade, and maintain core stack components, runtime environments, and third-party libraries to latest LTS or stable releases.
- Systematically refactor breaking changes, API migrations, and deprecation notices across the codebase without suppressing warnings.
- Validate every upgrade via the mandatory quality pipeline: build (`npm run build`), typecheck (`npm run typecheck`), lint (`npm run lint`), and unit tests with >= 90% per-file coverage (`npm run test:coverage`).
- Guarantee zero regressions before finalizing updates.

## 15. Automated Performance Optimization Policy
- Proactively audit latency bottlenecks and critical render paths across services.
- Implement code-splitting and lazy-loading (`next/dynamic`) for non-critical components.
- Apply resource caching across API and database queries with TTL and automated invalidation.
- Validate improvements against bundle size (<120 kB shared JS) and latency benchmarks.
- Maintain >= 90% unit test coverage individually on all performance modules.

## 16. Automated Log Error Monitoring & Resolution Policy
- Continuously monitor structured log files (`logs/app.log`, `logs/error.log`) and runtime error traces.
- Diagnose underlying bugs, uncaught rejections, and schema errors, implementing permanent verified fixes.
- Validate all bug fixes through linting, typechecking, and full test suite execution with >= 90% per-file coverage.

## 17. Automated Warning Resolution Policy
- Automatically identify, analyze, and resolve compiler, linter, runtime, and dependency warnings across the repository.
- Apply safe refactoring for deprecation notices, unused imports, type mismatches, and syntax warnings without suppressing warnings with comments.
- Validate fixes via `npm run typecheck`, `npm run lint`, and unit tests with >= 90% per-file code coverage.

